REVERTED — task 1567 phase 4, 2026-09-27. Do NOT re-apply as-is.

Attempted fix for goal 2's "hide the native window title" requirement. Added
Qt::FramelessWindowHint to the top-level document window's flags on
__EMSCRIPTEN__, so the synthetic Qt title bar (app icon + "Untitled 1 —
LibreOfficeDev Writer" + min/max/close, confirmed real and Qt-painted, not an
OS decoration, via evidence/screenshots/probe-chrome3-tofront.png) would stop
being drawn.

Built successfully (make -j12, ~5 min incremental) and DID remove the title
bar (confirmed: evidence/screenshots/phase4-chrome-writer.png before revert
showed no title bar). But it broke something more important: the mechanism
this whole product depends on for switching the visible document (a document
opened via loadComponentFromURL gets its OWN frame — confirmed
XFramesSupplier.getFrames() goes 1 -> 2 — and does not become visible on the
single shared #qtcanvas until XTopWindow::toFront() + XWindow::setVisible(true)
are called on it) stopped working with the frameless flag applied. Proven by
running the EXACT SAME toFront/setVisible calls (evidence/probe-chrome3.js)
against the frameless build: frame count still goes 1 -> 2, the calls throw no
exception, but the Start Center stays the visible frame regardless (screenshot
unchanged from before the calls). The same calls worked correctly on the
non-frameless build (both before this patch, and confirmed again after
reverting it).

Working theory (not confirmed further — reverted before investigating why):
Qt's Emscripten QPA platform plugin's logic for which top-level QMainWindow
owns the single OFFSCREENCANVAS surface likely keys off the window's flags/type
in a way that excludes a frameless window from being treated as a
compositor-eligible root surface, or from receiving the "raise" it needs.

Decision: reverted (git checkout -- vcl/qt5/QtFrame.cxx on the Legion tree,
confirmed via `git status --short` showing the file no longer modified) rather
than ship a version where opening a document silently leaves the Start Center
on screen — that is a total product break, worse than a visible title bar.
Rebuilt without this patch; the uno_scripts --post-js fix (0001-0004,
static/emscripten/bb-uno-scripts-default.js) is untouched and still applied.

The ORIGINAL diff (for whoever picks this up and wants to debug the QPA
compositing question properly, e.g. by reading
qtbase/src/plugins/platforms/wasm/ or instrumenting which QWindow the
canvas paints from):

--- a/vcl/qt5/QtFrame.cxx
+++ b/vcl/qt5/QtFrame.cxx
@@ -119,7 +119,22 @@ QtFrame::QtFrame(QtFrame* pParent, SalFrameStyleFlags nStyle)
         else if (nStyle & SalFrameStyleFlags::DIALOG || m_pParent)
             aWinFlags = Qt::Dialog;
         else
+#if defined __EMSCRIPTEN__
+            // Beebeeb office fork (task 1567 phase 4): this target has no real OS
+            // window manager at all (a browser canvas, not a desktop) -- Qt's own
+            // QMainWindow paints a SYNTHETIC title bar (app icon + title text +
+            // min/max/close) for a bare Qt::Window on a WM-less platform, which is
+            // exactly the chrome a host page embedding this canvas replaces with its
+            // own (task 1567 goal 2: "only the document area"). This is a Qt window-
+            // flag decision made at frame construction, not a UI element a
+            // LayoutManager/registrymodifications.xcu default can hide -- confirmed
+            // by screenshot (evidence/screenshots/probe-chrome3-tofront.png) that the
+            // title bar survives XLayoutManager::setVisible(false), which correctly
+            // hides the menubar/toolbars/statusbar/sidebar it DOES own.
+            aWinFlags = Qt::Window | Qt::FramelessWindowHint;
+#else
             aWinFlags = Qt::Window;
+#endif
     }

Status: task 1567's window-title hiding requirement is OPEN, not resolved.
The engine still shows a real Qt-painted title bar on every document window.
Next attempt should investigate the Qt WASM QPA's window-activation/compositing
code directly rather than guessing at flag combinations, or consider a
CSS-only mitigation on the host page (crop/mask a fixed-height strip if the
bar's height is stable) as a product-level workaround instead of an engine fix.
